## Push to GitHub (Fork Workflow)

### Important: Read Guidelines First

**Before pushing, read these in the main repo:**
- **CONTRIBUTING.md** - How to contribute technically
- **CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md** - Community behavior standards

**Why?** Shows you understand open source etiquette and DeepChem's specific requirements.

### Create Branch (Use CLI - NOT GitHub Website)

**CLI is faster and standard practice:**
```bash
# Navigate to repo
cd C:\Users\Ayman\OneDrive\Desktop\Projects\CONTRIBUTION_OpenSource\deepchem

# Create feature branch locally
git checkout -b gsoc-fem-demo

# Organize files
mkdir -p gsoc-2026-demo/implementations
cp simple_fem_1d.py heat_conduction.py mesh_2d.py gsoc-2026-demo/implementations/
mkdir -p gsoc-2026-demo/validation
cp validate_*.py gsoc-2026-demo/validation/

# Commit
git add gsoc-2026-demo/
git commit -m "Add GSOC 2026 FEM implementations

- 1D Poisson solver
- Heat conduction inverse problem
- 2D triangular mesh
- Validation scripts with convergence tests
- All core implementations under 50 lines"

# Push to your fork (https://github.com/amugoodbad229/deepchem.git)
git push origin gsoc-fem-demo
```

**Don't use GitHub website to create branches** - CLI is more efficient and standard.

